WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:06.539
When Oliver awoke the next morning, he was surprised to find a new pair of shoes on the floor next to his bed.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:13.713
He hoped this meant that Fagin was going to release him. But his hope disappeared as soon as he sat down to breakfast.

3
00:00:13.714 --> 00:00:18.959
"You're going to be taken to the residence of Bill Sikes tonight, my dear," said Fagin.

4
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.031
"To . . . to . . . stay there, sir?" Oliver asked anxiously.

5
00:00:24.032 --> 00:00:38.023
"No, no, my dear. We wouldn't like to lose you. Don't be afraid, Oliver. You'll come back to us again. We wouldn't be so cruel as to send you away. Oh no."

6
00:00:38.024 --> 00:00:42.110
The old man was stooping over the fire, toasting a piece of bread.

7
00:00:42.111 --> 00:00:47.007
He turned around and looked at Oliver as if he knew that the boy wanted to escape.

8
00:00:47.008 --> 00:00:51.047
"I suppose you want to know why you're going to Bill's."

9
00:00:51.048 --> 00:00:53.587
Fagin fixed his eyes on Oliver.

10
00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:55.261
"Yes, sir."

11
00:00:55.262 --> 00:00:57.429
"Why do you think you're going?"

12
00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:00.755
"Indeed I don't know, sir," replied Oliver.

13
00:01:00.756 --> 00:01:06.921
"Bah!" said Fagin, turning back to the fire. "Wait until Bill tells you then."

14
00:01:06.922 --> 00:01:13.818
For the rest of the day, Fagin was in a sour and silent mood. In the evening he prepared to go out.

15
00:01:13.819 --> 00:01:22.564
"You may burn a candle," he said, putting one on the table. "And here's a book for you to read until they come to fetch you. Good night."

16
00:01:22.565 --> 00:01:25.413
"Good night!" Oliver said softly.

17
00:01:25.414 --> 00:01:39.548
Fagin started to leave, but then he turned around. "Take heed, Oliver! Bill Sikes is a dangerous man. Do whatever he tells you to do. And no matter what happens, say nothing."

18
00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:42.469
The old man disappeared.

19
00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:48.135
Oliver sat with his head in his hands, pondering with a trembling heart the words he had just heard.

20
00:01:48.136 --> 00:01:55.437
At last he picked up the book and began to read. It was a history of the lives and trials of great criminals.

21
00:01:55.438 --> 00:02:01.349
And it was so frightening that Oliver slammed the book shut. He heard a rustling sound.

22
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:06.017
"Who's there?" he cried, catching sight of a figure standing by the door.

23
00:02:06.018 --> 00:02:09.183
"Only me," said a tremulous voice.

24
00:02:09.184 --> 00:02:13.596
Oliver raised the candle and saw Nancy, looking pale and ill.

25
00:02:13.597 --> 00:02:20.279
"Has anything happened?" asked Oliver. "Can I help you? I will if I can."

26
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.639
"God forgive me!" exclaimed the young woman.

27
00:02:23.663 --> 00:02:25.930
"What? Why?"

28
00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:32.758
Nancy struggled to compose herself. "Oh, it's nothing. Are you ready to go with me to Bill?"

29
00:02:32.782 --> 00:02:35.081
"What for?"

30
00:02:35.081 --> 00:02:40.572
Nancy avoided looking Oliver in the eye. "Oh, for no harm."

31
00:02:40.573 --> 00:02:42.675
"I don't believe it."

32
00:02:42.676 --> 00:02:47.072
"Fine, have it your way. For no good then."

33
00:02:47.073 --> 00:02:52.913
Suddenly she stooped over him and whispered, "I saved you from harm once, and I will again.

34
00:02:52.914 --> 00:03:01.643
This is not the time to try to escape. If you do, you may be the cause of my death. Now come with me and be quiet."

35
00:03:01.644 --> 00:03:09.818
She held out her hand, and Oliver instinctively took it. A carriage was waiting outside the house, and they quickly climbed into it.

36
00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:14.819
Within a few minutes, they were at the house that Fagin had visited the previous evening.

37
00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:20.128
"So you've got the kid," said Sikes when they arrived. "Did he come quietly?"

38
00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:22.961
"Quiet as a lamb," said Nancy.

39
00:03:22.985 --> 00:03:24.954
"Good."

40
00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:33.961
He pulled off Oliver's cap and threw it into a corner. Then taking Oliver by the shoulder, he sat down at the table and stood the boy in front of him.

41
00:03:33.962 --> 00:03:37.120
"If you speak a word when you're out with me . . ."

42
00:03:37.121 --> 00:03:41.707
Sikes went on to threaten Oliver, who winced as he tried not to listen.

43
00:03:41.708 --> 00:03:45.644
He didn't need to hear the words to know that his life was in danger.

44
00:03:45.645 --> 00:03:49.858
Oliver didn't have much appetite for supper and went to bed soon after.

45
00:03:49.859 --> 00:03:56.096
The next morning Nancy called him at five o'clock. Rain was beating against the windowpanes.

46
00:03:56.097 --> 00:03:59.945
After a quick breakfast, Sikes and Oliver headed out.

47
00:03:59.946 --> 00:04:05.604
They were on the road all day, traveling on foot and by wagon for more than 25 miles.

48
00:04:05.605 --> 00:04:13.771
By seven o'clock that night, they stood before a ruined and decayed house. It was dark and appeared uninhabited.

49
00:04:13.772 --> 00:04:18.088
But Sikes opened the door and found Toby Crackit sitting by the fire.

50
00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:25.921
"Bill, I'm glad to see you!" said Crackit. "I was afraid you'd given up. But who's this?"

51
00:04:25.922 --> 00:04:30.834
"One of Fagin's boys," said Sikes, pulling a chair toward the fire.

52
00:04:30.835 --> 00:04:38.572
"What an innocent face!" said Crackit. "That'll be invaluable for picking old ladies' pockets at church."

53
00:04:38.573 --> 00:04:45.231
"Enough of that. Give us something to eat and drink. It'll soon be time to go out again."

54
00:04:45.232 --> 00:04:52.802
The men ate heartily, but Oliver could only force down a small crust of bread. Then they all dozed in front of the fire.

55
00:04:52.803 --> 00:04:57.064
Suddenly Toby Crackit declared it was half past one o'clock.

56
00:04:57.065 --> 00:05:01.366
In an instant Sikes jumped up and began preparations to go out.

57
00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:08.295
Wrapping themselves in large scarves and heavy overcoats, they gathered up an odd assortment of tools.

58
00:05:08.296 --> 00:05:14.366
The two robbers went out the door with Oliver between them. It was now intensely dark.

59
00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:20.382
The fog was heavy, and the air was so cold and damp that Oliver's hair and eyebrows froze.

60
00:05:20.383 --> 00:05:24.112
They passed through the town of Chertsey and turned up a road.

61
00:05:24.113 --> 00:05:31.707
Soon they came to a house surrounded by a wall. Crackit went over first, and then Sikes gave Oliver a boost.

62
00:05:31.708 --> 00:05:40.533
And now, for the first time, Oliver realized that they were going to rob the house. Filled with terror, he sank to his knees.

63
00:05:40.534 --> 00:05:50.009
"Oh, let me go!" he pleaded. "I'll never come near London, never, never! Have mercy on me and don't make me steal."

64
00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:55.850
"Get up!" muttered Sikes, trembling with rage. "And do what I tell you."

65
00:05:55.851 --> 00:06:01.485
They crept toward the back of the house, where a little window was covered by a shutter.

66
00:06:01.486 --> 00:06:07.572
While Crackit used a crowbar to pry off the shutter, Sikes whispered instructions in Oliver's ear.

67
00:06:07.573 --> 00:06:15.064
His job was to go through the window, walk along a hallway to the front of the house, and open the door for Sikes and Crackit.

68
00:06:15.065 --> 00:06:21.818
Sikes boosted Oliver up to the window and handed him a lantern. "Here, take this light."

69
00:06:21.819 --> 00:06:27.556
Oliver landed in a small room. Up ahead he could see a flight of stairs.

70
00:06:27.557 --> 00:06:35.223
And suddenly he decided to warn the inhabitants, no matter what happened to him. He started to run toward the stairs.

71
00:06:35.224 --> 00:06:39.263
"Wait!" called Sikes. "Come back!"

72
00:06:39.287 --> 00:06:42.970
Startled, Oliver dropped the lantern with a loud clatter.

73
00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:45.697
"Who's that?" called a voice.

74
00:06:45.721 --> 00:06:50.237
A vision of two men at the top of the stairs swam before Oliver's eyes.

75
00:06:50.237 --> 00:06:55.967
Then one of them fired a gun, and Oliver fell, wounded.

